<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Graubünden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Grisons, Grigioni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Graubünden, known in French and English as the Grisons, is the largest and easternmost canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a>. It grew from the Three Leagues, an alliance of the Grey League, the League of God's House and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a>, which ruled the area as a free state from the 15th century. It became a Swiss canton in 1803 and is the only trilingual canton, using German, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romansh">Romansh</a> and Italian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/graubunden.jpg" alt="Graubünden" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: de:Benutzer:Breeze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The canton lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and, unlike any other Swiss canton, extends fully on both sides of the main Alpine barrier, from the northern plains at Maienfeld to the southern plains at Roveredo. Much of it belongs to neither side: the Engadin is a large inner Alpine valley that drains through the Inn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Parts of the canton lie in four of the country's five drainage basins, drained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the Po, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> and the Inn. Its heraldic symbol is the Alpine ibex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The deep valleys were first settled by the Raetians. Archaeological evidence of settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a> goes back to the Pfyn culture, 3900 to 3500 BCE. Most of the land became part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, established in 15 BCE, with Chur as Curia, and later lay in the territory of the diocese of Chur.</p><p>In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> three leagues formed. The League of God's House was founded in 1367 to resist the rising power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a>. The Grey League followed in 1395 in the Upper Rhine valley, named for the homespun grey clothes of its people, and that name was used exclusively after 16 March 1424. In 1436 the people of ten bailiwicks of the former Toggenburg countship, whose dynasty had died out, formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a>. An alliance of 1471 produced the Three Leagues, which before the end of the 15th century became an ally of the Old Swiss Confederacy. The Grey League's name gave the canton its German name, meaning the Grey Leagues.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a> is the capital and the only sizable city; most people live in mountain valleys, and the canton has 200,096 inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romansh">Romansh</a> has official status here and has been a national language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> since 1938; in 1996 it was declared an official language of the Confederation. It consists of five dialect groups, with a common written form, Rumantsch Grischun. Since the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it has lost more than half its original territory, which included Liechtenstein and parts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p>Three World Heritage Sites lie in the canton: the Benedictine Convent of Saint John, the Swiss Tectonic Arena Sardona and the Rhaetian Railway in the Albula and Bernina landscapes. The church of Zillis holds a Romanesque painted ceiling added about 1130, and the canton has many medieval castles, among them Tarasp in the Engadin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grisons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It restored a confederation of largely self-governing cantons, now nineteen: the thirteen members of the Old Swiss Confederacy and six new cantons formed from former subject and allied lands, namely St. Gallen, <b>Graubünden</b>, Aargau, Thurgau, Ticino and Vaud.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The league's name survives in <b>Graubünden</b>, the canton's German name.</blockquote><a href="/state/grey-league">Grey League</a></li><li><blockquote>The league remained part of the Three Leagues until 1799, when French forces attached the <b>Grisons</b> to the Helvetic Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-gods-house">League of God's House</a></li><li><blockquote>The league ended with the Three Leagues when the <b>Grisons</b> were attached to the Helvetic Republic in 1799, and it became part of the canton of Graubünden in 1803.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Grisons</b> troubles of 1618 to 1639, a civil war entangled with Spanish and French interests, tore the state apart.</blockquote><a href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a></li></ul></section>
